# Getting started

This Lookbook documents the component library for NHS Rails pages:
what each component is for, when to reach for it, and the rules to
follow when composing pages — for human developers and LLM agents
alike. Previews show every component live; these docs pages carry the
guidance.

## How this library works

The library is plain Rails view code on top of
[basecoat](https://basecoatui.com) — no view-component framework, no
React, no build-time component compiler.

- **Styling** comes from `basecoat-css` plus Tailwind, imported in
  `app/assets/tailwind/application.css`. Clinical state colour tokens
  (warning, critical, stale, info, success) live in
  `app/assets/tailwind/theme.css`. Where a component needs styling
  basecoat does not ship, it gets its own file in
  `app/assets/tailwind/components/<name>.css`, registered in
  `application.css`.
- **Behaviour** comes from basecoat's bundle, imported from the
  `basecoat-css` npm package (select, command, toast) and small
  Stimulus controllers in `app/javascript/controllers`. Native browser behaviour is preferred
  over custom JavaScript: `<details>` for disclosure, `<dialog>` for
  overlays, native form controls throughout.
- **Markup** is ERB partials in `app/views/gesso/components/`, one component
  per partial.

## Partials and locals

Each component is rendered through a `render_<component>` view helper
(`Gesso::Components::<Name>Helper`) that owns its logic and validation and
renders a partial named `_<component>.html.erb`. Conventions for those
partials:

- A strict `locals: (...)` signature on the first line declares
  every accepted local. Locals without a default are required; optional
  ones carry their default in the signature. Passing an unknown local —
  or omitting a required one — raises, so the contract is enforced.
- A `<%%# ... %>` doc comment below the signature describes each param
  and its accepted values.
- A `classes` local lets callers append extra CSS classes; partials
  never accept raw style attributes.
- Components that wrap content take a block and `yield` it (for
  example the button label, card body, dialog body).

```erb
<%%= render_card(title: "Patient") do %>
  Card body content
<%% end %>
```

## Previews

Every component has a Lookbook preview in
`spec/components/previews/<name>_preview.rb`, a `Lookbook::Preview`
subclass with:

- a `@label` annotation naming the component in the sidebar,
- a `playground` scenario with `@param` annotations so every param can
  be exercised interactively,
- one named scenario per variant or notable state.

Open a preview alongside a component's guidance page (under
*Components* in the sidebar) to see the rules applied to live
rendering.

## Specs

Each component carries a request spec in `spec/components/<name>_spec.rb`
(`type: :request`) that renders scenarios through the mounted preview
route and asserts on the DOM:

```ruby
get "/lookbook/preview/button/primary"
page = Capybara.string(response.body)
expect(page).to have_css("button.btn[data-variant='primary']")
```

Docs pages follow the same pattern from `spec/docs/`, asserting each
page renders with its key headings. Any change to a component ships
with its preview and spec in the same commit.
